{"author":{"id":"2462ded6f56967dde059994c39a506c79e39e5dfd10c5a07f07a23a5652a8353"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1774367393,"nonce":"DQwqmLUFqKARcPwJ3HyMFgynwnA=","title":"Max-turns exit during review should fall back to human review","message":"When a review session exits with error_max_turns (hit the 31-turn limit), the watcher sets it to suspended:unexpected. This leaves the coder session stuck in reviewing status with no way forward.\n\nContext: In review-9d165d4ba005, the reviewer finished its actual review work (all tests passed, verdict ready) but burned remaining turns trying to close a git-bug issue with wrong syntax. The max-turns exit was treated as unexpected.\n\nFix: In SessionRunner or post_run.py, when a review session exits with max-turns:\n- Check if a verdict was already emitted in the conversation (scan for @nightshift approve/revise)\n- If yes: treat as successful completion, transition to waiting:review so the verdict handler picks it up\n- If no: fall back to waiting:human-review on the coder session (same as the review-failed path from 4de9c11)\n\nThis is different from coder max-turns (which triggers auto-resume). Review sessions should not auto-resume on max-turns — they should either deliver their verdict or yield to a human.","files":null}]}